#!/bin/sh
# youcoded-askpass — the SUDO_ASKPASS target for native `sudo` invocations.
# sudo execve()s this file directly (never through a shell you'd otherwise
# trust) whenever it has no terminal, and reads whatever it prints to stdout
# as the password (sudo(8), src/tgetpass.c). Every native Bash command runs
# under setsid with no controlling terminal, so this is what actually answers
# `sudo` for the model. See docs/active/specs/2026-09-26-admin-password-
# technical-design.md §2.1, and design reviews 1 (D1) and 2 (E1).
#
# NO OTHER LOGIC BELONGS HERE. Everything downstream (verifying who is
# allowed to receive a password, reading it back from the app) happens only
# after this file hands off to askpass.cjs under a scrubbed environment —
# adding anything else here would run before that scrub, i.e. in an
# environment this design treats as hostile by construction.

# WHY ulimit -c 0 first, before anything else: `env -i` below scrubs
# environment VARIABLES only. Resource limits are a separate kernel
# namespace and pass unchanged through fork()+exec() (review 2, E1) — a
# command run earlier in the same shell/session (`ulimit -c unlimited`,
# which is session state, not per-command) could otherwise leave this
# process's core-dump limit uncapped, and a same-uid SIGQUIT/SIGABRT/SIGSEGV
# aimed at askpass.cjs (no ptrace, no ancestry needed — same-uid signal
# delivery is all it takes) could dump the just-received plaintext password
# to a core file the model then reads back with its own tools. This is a
# shell builtin, costs nothing, and is not undone by anything below.
ulimit -c 0

# WHY resolve our own directory from $0, never from an inherited env var:
# sudo execve()s this script by the absolute path it already resolved from
# SUDO_ASKPASS, so $0 IS that absolute path — finding our sibling
# askpass.cjs needs nothing from the environment sudo handed us, trusted or
# not. Trusting the environment for anything that feeds the verified helper
# is exactly what review 1 (D1) says never to do.
#
# WHY ${0%/*} and not `dirname "$0"` (task-2 review, T2-2, High): `dirname`
# is NOT a shell builtin — it's an external binary, looked up through
# PATH, and at this exact point in the script PATH is STILL the
# model's own unsanitised, inherited value (`env -i` hasn't run yet, on
# purpose: it has to run last). A PATH entry planted ahead of the real
# `dirname` (trivial — the model already has arbitrary shell execution)
# would make $dir, and therefore the very file this script `exec`s as
# "askpass.cjs", attacker-chosen, before ANY hardening (which lives
# entirely inside askpass.cjs) has run. `${0%/*}` is POSIX parameter
# expansion — a shell syntax construct, not a command, so nothing is
# looked up through PATH at all. It strips the shortest trailing "/*"
# from $0; if $0 has no "/" at all, the expansion leaves $0 unchanged
# (nothing matched), which the `case` below turns into "the current
# directory" — the same fallback `dirname` would have reported.
dir=${0%/*}
case $0 in
  */*) ;;
  *) dir=. ;;
esac

# WHY unset CDPATH before cd: CDPATH only redirects a `cd` given a
# relative, non-"."/".."-prefixed argument — sudo always execs this
# script by an absolute path so $dir is normally already absolute and
# CDPATH can't apply, but nothing here should depend on remembering
# that; unsetting it removes the possibility outright rather than
# relying on an invariant elsewhere.
unset CDPATH

# WHY `cd -P -- "$dir" && pwd -P`, both POSIX SHELL BUILTINS (no PATH
# lookup, same reasoning as the `${0%/*}` fix above) rather than a
# `realpath` binary: not every POSIX sh environment ships `realpath` on
# PATH, but `cd`+`pwd` are required builtins. `-P` on `cd` resolves every
# symlink component (an AppImage mount, a symlinked worktree checkout)
# as it changes directory, which is what "realpath of dir" means below;
# `pwd -P` then reports that already-resolved physical path. `--` stops
# a directory name that happens to start with `-` from being read as an
# option.
dir=$(cd -P -- "$dir" && pwd -P)

# WHY exec, never a plain call: this must be the LAST process in the chain.
# AskpassServer's identity check (design §3.1) reads the real executable,
# exact argv and exact environment of the pid that connects to the socket —
# if this shell stayed alive as a parent instead of being replaced, the
# process that actually connects would be one hop further from what §3.1
# expects, and nothing in the design accounts for that hop.
#
# WHY env -i is load-bearing (review 1, D1): sudo forks this script with ITS
# OWN unsanitised process environment, which — because env_reset only
# scrubs the environment handed to the eventual AUTHORIZED command, never
# sudo's own process env used to spawn askpass — is transitively the
# untrusted Bash command's own environment. Without `env -i`, a command
# could set NODE_OPTIONS=--require=evil.js (directly, or via a
# `persistent_env` left over from an earlier call) ahead of the approved
# sudo, and that variable would ride sudo's fork straight into the Node
# runtime that ends up holding the real password — passing every one of
# §3.1's checks trivially, because the injected code runs INSIDE the exact
# verified binary/argv. `env -i` drops everything except the two names
# given here, so nothing else survives that fork.
exec /usr/bin/env -i ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 YOUCODED_ASKPASS_SOCKET="$YOUCODED_ASKPASS_SOCKET" "$YOUCODED_ASKPASS_RUNTIME" "$dir/askpass.cjs"
